A||||10||mrfixit58||FORMULA nose job?||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||01-31-2001||08:17 AM||||I'm not real familiar with the 88 Formulas. Is the nose piece unique to the Formula? If so, what are the major differences between the Formula and the coupe? Is the round molding around the nose a good indicator?<P>Thanks, <BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||01-31-2001||08:21 AM||||The 88 Formula nosepiece and 87-88 Coupe nosepieces are identical.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Stoic||01-31-2001||10:18 AM||||Actually, correct me if I'm wrong but I believe the formulas have an air dam under the nose, where as the coupes have the plain jane spoiler underneath. (If that makes sense) <P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||6
Z||000003||Raydar||01-31-2001||10:22 AM||||88 coupe and 88 Formula nose are the same. At least <I>mine</I> are. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||01-31-2001||10:52 AM||||Thanks guys. The nose piece has round molding. I thought they used the ribbed molding on everything before 88 except the GT. ChadMan, Are yo sure the 87 had round molding?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>The reason I ask is that I'm thinking of buying an 86 SE and it has this nose one it. The rest of the car has the grooved molding. I was wondering if the nose is worth anything (e.g., are they hard to come by in good shape?).<P>Any help is appreciated,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000005||mrfiero||01-31-2001||01:41 PM||||All '87-'88 coupes and '88 Formulas share the same front fascia.......there is no difference between them.<P>All '87-'88 coupes, '86-'88 GTs and '88 Formulas have the smooth moldings......all other years/models have the ribbed.<P>The most interesting is the '87 SE.....it was  the only model in the '87 line up to have ribbed moldings. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||01-31-2001||01:45 PM||||FWIW... A guy in my area that parts out trashed Fieros says that the 87-88 nose is fairly uncommon. Don't know if that makes them worth any more, though.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000007||JC Webb||01-31-2001||02:07 PM||||I have an 86 SE 2M6 with ribbed molding. Can I replace it with the newer smooth molding?<BR>Thanks, John C. Webb||||reg||5
Z||000008||mrfixit58||01-31-2001||03:13 PM||||Thanks guys,<P>At least if I buy it I'll know what I have.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000009||mrfiero||01-31-2001||05:07 PM||||JC Webb,<P>Yes, you can add the smooth moling to your SE.  The hard part is the rear fascia.  The molding is actually part of the fascia, so you will need to buy one from an '86-'88 GT.  The front fascia from your SE will need to be slightly modified to accept the smooth style sidemarker lights.  You will need to carefully cut off the small ribbed section of molding that is molded into the fascia.  If you trim it right the sidemarker will cover it up and no one will know.<BR>The rest of the moldings just insall in place of the ribbed ones. You will likely have to buy new clips, since they usually break when reoving the molding.  <P>It may cost you more to do this than you're willing to pay, but it is possible and many have already done it.<BR>   ||||reg||10
Z||000010||rockcrawl||01-31-2001||09:09 PM||||Wow, I was just thinking about this stuff today. I figured the '87 SE was unique in that it was the only Aero-bodied notchback with round moulding, but according to what I read here there was never an Aero-body notchback with round moulding.<P> Cool, I'm going to build one and see if anybody notices!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||12
